<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agnes Bernauer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1410–<a href="/years/1435">1435</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Agnes Bernauer was the daughter of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> barber-surgeon who became the wife, probably by secret marriage, of Albert, heir to the Duchy of Bavaria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>. His father, Duke Ernest, saw the match as a threat to the dynasty; while Albert was away he had her condemned, partly on charges of witchcraft, and drowned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straubing">Straubing</a> in 1435. Her story inspired plays by Friedrich Hebbel and others.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agnes-bernauer.jpg" alt="Agnes Bernauer" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agnes_Bernauer_Ducissa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nothing is known of Agnes Bernauer's childhood and youth, and the existence of the barber-surgeon Kaspar Bernauer, traditionally named as her father, has not been proved. Because Albert took part in a tournament at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> in February 1428, it is generally assumed that he met her there and soon brought her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>; a Munich tax roll of 1428 lists a pernawin in his household, probably Agnes.</p><p>By the summer of 1432 at the latest she was an integral part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> court. She took part in the capture of the robber baron Münnhauser, who had fled to the Old Court in Munich, and her self-assured manner annoyed Albert's sister, the Palatine Countess Beatrix. There is no concrete evidence of a marriage ceremony. From 1433 Albert often stayed at Blutenburg Castle, and the sale of two nearby properties to Agnes suggests that the couple lived there together; no descendants are known.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Duke Ernest, fearing for the succession of his only son, had Agnes arrested while Albert was at a hunt arranged by his relative Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria-landshut">Bavaria-Landshut</a>, and she was drowned near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straubing">Straubing</a> on 12 October 1435. Albert went to Duke Louis VII at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a>, but after a few months was reconciled with his father and in November 1436 married Anna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>. The feared war between father and son never came; Emperor Sigismund may have restrained Albert.</p><p>In December 1435 Albert endowed a perpetual mass for Agnes in the Carmelite cloister at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straubing">Straubing</a>, and in 1436 his father built an Agnes Bernauer Chapel in the cemetery of St. Peter, probably to appease him. Its red marble tombstone shows her almost life-size, holding a rosary, with two small dogs at her feet; her grave has never been found. The love story became a staple of folk songs and inspired works from Joseph August Graf von Toerring's play of 1780 to Hebbel's tragedy of 1855 and Carl Orff's Die Bernauerin of 1944.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Agnes Bernauer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke Ernest had <b>Agnes Bernauer</b>, the commoner wife of his son Albert III, drowned at Straubing in 1435.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-munich">Duchy of Bavaria-Munich</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straubing">Straubing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.37,10.9&amp;year=1426" aria-label="Augsburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4803 472 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Agnes Bernauer"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5253 742h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5253 742h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.37,10.9&amp;year=1426">What was here in 1426? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
